<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Mapungubwe</h1><p class="st-meta">1075 CE – 1220 CE (approx.) · capital Mapungubwe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Southern Africa's first class-divided kingdom, ruled from a hilltop above the Limpopo and famed for its golden rhinoceros.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The kingdom grew out of the earlier settlement at K2 (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambandyanalo">Bambandyanalo</a>) near the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shashe-river">Shashe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> rivers. The ruling group later moved onto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapungubwe-hill">Mapungubwe Hill</a>, separating itself physically from commoners below. Wealth came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, farming on the floodplain and a growing trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and gold with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili merchants</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Archaeology is the only source. The elite lived and were buried on the hilltop with gold-foil objects, including the famous golden rhinoceros, while ordinary people lived in the valley. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gla">Glass</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, Chinese celadon and locally worked gold show a link through coastal ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> to the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> economy. Sacred leadership and rainmaking probably underpinned royal authority.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Dates differ between chronologies: older ones place the kingdom between about 1075 and 1220, while more recent research dates the hilltop capital to about 1220-1290. Mapungubwe was then abandoned, possibly as a cooler, drier climate undermined floodplain farming. Power and trade shifted north to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-zimbabwe">Kingdom of Zimbabwe</a>, which inherited many of its institutions. The site is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage</a> landscape in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">South Africa</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-mapungubwe.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Mapungubwe" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: JJ van Zyl · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MapungubweHill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1075 CE – 1220 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mapungubwe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional African religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Early Shona (probably)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>South Africa, Zimbabwe, Botswana</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mapungubwe</dd></dl></aside></div>
